codex
The bare loop reached a script-owned `cap_bound`: `easy` and `medium` were solved 2/3, and `hard` was solved 1/3. Because the ladder changed only witness length, the harness correctly refused to call this a paper failure. I’m now applying the required fixed-length hardening axis: keep the 100-ID witness and 299-XOR recurrence fixed, but add independently sampled ternary parity blocks around the planted tight cycle. These are same-distribution, satisfied constraints; they grow the structural haystack without growing the answer.
2026-09-04T23:03:05.208085Z ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=apply_patch verification failed: Failed to find expected lines in /home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.01757/gen_1604_01757.py:
The compact no-tool route recognizes that the
ternary equation supports are the length-three windows of a hidden cycle; the
binary equations merely display some adjacent-row differences. Adjacent
ternary equations give x_i XOR x_(i+3)=r_i XOR r_(i+1); since gcd(n,3)=1, one
traversal expresses every bit relative to one bit, and one original equation
fixes it. At n=100 this is 3n-1=299 exact XORs, versus the measured elimination
cost recorded by selftest.
